<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khorramshahr</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mohammerah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khorramshahr, formerly Mohammerah, is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a> province, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, at the meeting of the Karun river and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatt-al-arab">Shatt al-Arab</a>. It was developed in the early 19th century by the Muhaysin branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kab">Banu Ka'b</a>, and under Sheikh Khaz'al it was the seat of a largely autonomous Arab sheikhdom until 1925. Iraqi forces captured it in 1980 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran-iraq-war">Iran–Iraq War</a>, and Iran retook it in May 1982.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khorramshahr.jpg" alt="Khorramshahr" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohammad Amin Ansari · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nowruz_2018_in_Khorramshahr_(13970109000585636579510470063573_33197).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
